            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000007",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000008",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-000000000009",
            "visible": true,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000a",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000b",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000c",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          },
          {
            "targetLayer": "2c7f4b20-0000-4000-8000-00000000000d",
            "visible": false,
            "opacity": 1.0
          }
        ],
        "content": {
          "attachments": {},
          "contentMode": "map",
          "image": "",
          "imageCaption": "",
          "markdown": "**Two more things this map cannot tell you, and leaves empty rather than fills\nin.**\n\n**Where the damage ends.** The volunteer record stops at **km\n78** — which is where one mapping project ended and the next\nbegan, not where the flood did. The water ran on for another 130 km.\n\n**What the cloud hid.** Copernicus publish the ground they could not assess: for\none area that is **5.2 km²**, larger than the\ndamage they mapped inside it.\n\n*Draft. Arjun to rewrite.*\n\n**On the map:** grey is ground that could not be assessed · outlines are mapping projects\n",
          "paneAlignment": "start",
          "panelWidth": "34%",
          "title": "What is still not known"
        }
      }
    },
